  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\n- Mainly afternoon scattered showers and thunderstorms today\n\nThe 17Z surface analysis indicated a nearly stationary front \nextending through extreme SW Lower MI to north of Jackson and then \ncurving northeast towards the Thumb. Thunderstorms are expected to \nbe restricted mainly south and east of this front with severe \nconvection unlikely. Mid and upper cloudiness partly sourced from \nan MCS over Missouri is noted on the equatorward side of the polar \njet and this area of cloudiness is streaming across much of Lower \nMichigan at this time. This is limiting insolation and diurnal \ndestabilization. Moreover, local VWP profiles indicate weak shear. \nThis all supports the idea of disorganized and generally short-lived \nshowers and thunderstorms mainly this afternoon. Coverage will \nprobably be limited compared to previous days.\n\nAs noted in the aviation section of this forecast discussion, we \ncannot rule out some fog once again Friday morning.\n\n\n- Showers and thunderstorms Friday night, drier Saturday\n\nThere is agreement amount multiple deterministic models that a \nshortwave trough will swing through Friday night/early Saturday \ntimeframe with an uptick of convection. Some of the Machine Learning \nmodels hint at severe potential during this time. Other guidance \nshows convection dissipating before reaching Lower MI. That said, \nguidance like the 12Z HRRR does indicate sufficient thermodynamics \n(MUCAPE ~2000 J/kg and DCAPE > 800 J/kg) for damaging convective \nwind gusts even in the absence of organizing shear. If convection \nmanages to cross Lake Michigan relatively intact and move into this \nfavorable environment, that will significantly increase the chances \nfor strong/severe wind gusts.\n\nSomewhat implicit in this scenario is a subsequent largely dry \nSaturday afternoon/evening. That is what our forecast will \nadvertise, but with the same caveat that things can change due to \ntiming. \n\n\n- Showers and thunderstorms likely Monday or Monday night\n\nProbably the best chances for severe weather during this forecast cycle\nexist during this time frame. Medium range guidance generally agrees\nthat a shortwave trough within a band of stronger mid-level westerly\nflow will traverse the western Great Lakes. Shear will be substantially\nmore favorable for organized convection (for example 0-6km bulk\nvalues around 50 kt, 0-3km bulk values around 30 kt). Conditional\ninstability is less certain. It would appear that at least the \nsouthern forecast area could see MLCAPE exceeding 1000 J/kg, which\nwould be sufficient for severe weather given the kinematics. This\nis all subject to change, but certainly one of the most noteworthy\nthings to watch.\n\n\n- Continued chances for thunderstorms into mid-week\n\nAs noted previously, a persistent upper low in the vicinity of \nHudson Bay will keep deep cyclonic flow over the western Great Lakes \nwith several opportunities for precipitation as upper PV lobes \nrotate through.",
